Option 1: The Lambda Strategy (Simplest & Best for Streams)
---------------------------------------------------------------

Instead of forcing your rules to implement a rigid interface with fixed parameters, you can redefine your rules as simple Predicate<Product> factories. This decouples the rule initialization from the execution stream completely.

1. Define Rules as Functions that Accept Different Parameters
public class ProductRules {
    // This rule only cares about name and a case-sensitivity flag
    public static Predicate<Product> nameMatches(String searchName, boolean caseSensitive) {
        return product -> searchName == null || searchName.isEmpty() ||
               (caseSensitive ? product.name().equals(searchName) : product.name().equalsIgnoreCase(searchName));
    }

    // This rule only cares about min and max prices
    public static Predicate<Product> priceWithinRange(Integer min, Integer max) {
        return product -> {
            boolean matchesMin = (min == null || min == 0) || (product.price() >= min);
            boolean matchesMax = (max == null || max == 0) || (product.price() <= max);
            return matchesMin && matchesMax;
        };
    }

    // This rule cares about a completely different parameter: Inventory Stock
    public static Predicate<Product> hasMinimumStock(Integer requiredStock) {
        return product -> requiredStock == null || product.stockCount() >= requiredStock;
    }
}

2. Dynamically Assemble Only the Rules You Need
In your service layer, look at the incoming payload and add only the relevant rules to your pipeline list.
public List<Product> search(List<Product> products, String name, Integer min, Integer max, Integer minStock) {
    List<Predicate<Product>> activeFilters = new ArrayList<>();

    // Instantiate rules with their completely unique parameters
    if (name != null) activeFilters.add(ProductRules.nameMatches(name, false));
    if (min != null || max != null) activeFilters.add(ProductRules.priceWithinRange(min, max));
    if (minStock != null) activeFilters.add(ProductRules.hasMinimumStock(minStock));

    // Execute the stream naturally
    return products.stream()
        .filter(product -> activeFilters.stream().allMatch(filter -> filter.test(product)))
        .toList();
}


Option 2: The Command Pattern with an Open Context Map
------------------------------------------------------------

Instead of defining a rigid interface for your rules, you can use a command pattern where each rule is a command that takes a context map. This allows you to pass any number of parameters without changing the interface.

If you want to stick strictly to an Object-Oriented Class structure (like class MinPriceRule implements ProductRule), you can replace the rigid SearchCriteria with a generic, extensible RuleContext containing an open-ended map of values.

1. Define a Dynamic Context Object
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class RuleContext {
    private final Map<String, Object> parameters = new HashMap<>();

    public void set(String key, Object value) { parameters.put(key, value); }

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public <T> T get(String key, Class<T> type) {
        return (T) parameters.get(key);
    }
}

2. The Rule Interface Now Takes the Generic Context
interface ProductRule {
    boolean matches(Product product, RuleContext context);
}

3. Rules Extract Only Their Specific Parameters Safely
class NameRule implements ProductRule {
    public boolean matches(Product p, RuleContext ctx) {
        String name = ctx.get("searchName", String.class);
        Boolean caseSensitive = ctx.get("caseSensitive", Boolean.class);

        if (name == null) return true;
        return caseSensitive ? p.name().equals(name) : p.name().equalsIgnoreCase(name);
    }
}

class StockRule implements ProductRule {
    public boolean matches(Product p, RuleContext ctx) {
        Integer minStock = ctx.get("requiredStock", Integer.class);
        return minStock == null || p.stockCount() >= minStock;
    }
}


