When scaling to more than 10 rules, managing them manually via lists, nested ifs, or factories breaks down. It makes your service class bloated and hard to test.The industry standard for managing a large volume of dynamic business rules in Java or Kotlin is to use a Self-Registering Rule Engine managed by Spring Framework (Dependency Injection).By letting Spring find and manage your rules, you can add 10, 20, or 100 rules without changing a single line of execution code.

Step 1: Define a Scalable Rule Interface
Every rule needs to decide two things: when it should run (based on user input) and what logic to execute.
public interface ProductFilteringRule {
    /**
     * Checks the user's input parameters to see if this specific rule is active.
     */
    boolean isActive(SearchCriteria criteria);

    /**
     * Executes the actual filtering logic against a product.
     */
    boolean matches(Product product, SearchCriteria criteria);
}


Step 2: Write Small, Independent Rules (Spring Components)
Mark each rule with @Component. Spring will automatically find them and add them to a registry.
@Component
public class NameRule implements ProductFilteringRule {
    @Override
    public boolean isActive(SearchCriteria c) {
        return c.getName() != null && !c.getName().isEmpty();
    }

    @Override
    public boolean matches(Product p, SearchCriteria c) {
        return p.getName().equalsIgnoreCase(c.getName());
    }
}

@Component
public class MinPriceRule implements ProductFilteringRule {
    @Override
    public boolean isActive(SearchCriteria c) {
        return c.getMinPrice() != null && c.getMinPrice() > 0;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean matches(Product p, SearchCriteria c) {
        return p.getPrice() >= c.getMinPrice();
    }
}

@Component
public class CategoryRule implements ProductFilteringRule {
    @Override
    public boolean isActive(SearchCriteria c) {
        return c.getCategory() != null;
    }

    @Override
    public boolean matches(Product p, SearchCriteria c) {
        return p.getCategory().equalsIgnoreCase(c.getCategory());
    }
}
// Repeat this clean pattern for all 10+ rules (e.g., StockRule, RatingRule, BrandRule)


Step 3: Inject and Execute the Engine Natively
@Service
public class ProductSearchService {

    // Spring automatically discovers and injects ALL 10+ implemented rules here
    private final List<ProductFilteringRule> allRules;

    public ProductSearchService(List<ProductFilteringRule> allRules) {
        this.allRules = allRules;
    }

    public List<Product> search(List<Product> products, SearchCriteria criteria) {

        // 1. Pre-filter only the rules that the user actually provided inputs for
        List<ProductFilteringRule> activeRules = allRules.stream()
                .filter(rule -> rule.isActive(criteria))
                .toList();

        // 2. Stream through products and evaluate them only against active rules
        return products.stream()
                .filter(product -> activeRules.stream().allMatch(rule -> rule.matches(product, criteria)))
                .toList();
    }
}

Why this is perfect for 10+ Rules:
Open/Closed Principle: To add an 11th rule, you create a new class file with @Component. You do not touch ProductSearchService, SearchCriteria, or existing tests.
Performance: If a user only searches by name, the engine filters out the other 9+ inactive rules instantly. The product stream only executes the NameRule evaluation logic.
